  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>Max size: 450 mm SL (45 cm).</p><p>Difficulty: Moderate. Needs a very large tank and should be kept in a group.</p><p>The barbel steed, Hemibarbus labeo, is a cyprinid widespread in eastern Asia between the Yangtze and Amur river basins. A predominantly riverine fish of clear, well-oxygenated running water, it forms schools just above the substrate as an adult, and some populations make annual spawning migrations. Adults lack dark spots on the body and have a long snout and well-developed lips.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>Widespread in eastern Asia between the Yangtze and Amur river basins, including China, Taiwan, Korea, Mongolia, Russia, and Japan. May have been introduced to the Mekong basin in Laos.</p><p>Type locality is 'Transbaikalia, Dauria; Onon River, Russia [Upper Amur River]'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>Predominantly a riverine fish preferring clear, well-oxygenated, running waters with substrates of sand, gravel, rock or mud, where adults tend to form schools just above the substrate.</p><p>At least some populations undergo annual spawning migrations.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>Wild individuals are known to feed on benthic crustaceans, insects, molluscs, and other macroinvertebrates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>Sexually mature, nuptial males develop fine tubercules on the head and fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<p>This species can be distinguished from other members of the genus by the following combination of characters: absence of dark spots on body in adults; body elongate with slightly convex dorsal profile; head longer than body is deep; snout much longer than postorbital head length; lips well developed, lateral lobes of lower lip broad and thick with folds, median process small; barbel thickness shorter or equal to eye diameter; dorsal spine strong, ⅔ of HL; dorsal-fin origin closer to tip of snout than caudal-fin base; 15+ gill rakers; 6½ branched anal-fin rays; posterior simple dorsal-fin rays ossified and spinous.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemibarbus-labeo",
      "html": "<ul><li>Kottelat, M. (2013). The fishes of the inland waters of southeast Asia: a catalogue and core bibiography of the fishes known to occur in freshwaters, mangroves and estuaries. The Raffles Bulletin of Zoology Supplement 27, 1-663</li><li>Kottelat, M. (2006). Fishes of Mongolia. A check-list of the fishes known to occur in Mongolia with comments on systematics and nomenclature. The World Bank. Washington, DC., i-xi + 1-103</li><li>Bogutskaya, N. G., A. M. Naseka, S. V. Shedko, E. D. Vasil'eva and I. A. Chereshnev (2008). The fishes of the Amur River: updated check-list and zoogeography. Ichthyological Exploration of Freshwaters 19(4), 301-366</li><li>Lin, C-J., H-D. Lin, J-P. Wang, S-C. Chao and T-Y. Chiang (2010). Phylogeography of Hemibarbus labeo (Cyprinidae): secondary contact of ancient lineages of mtDNA. Zoologica Scripta 39(1), 22-35</li><li>Ho, H.-C. and K.-T. Shao (2011). Annotated checklist and type catalog of fish genera and species described from Taiwan. Zootaxa 2957, 1-74</li><li>Yue, P. Q. (1995). A revision of the cyprinid fishes of the genus Hemibarbus in China (Cypriniformes: Cyprinidae). Acta Zootaxonomica Sinica 20(1), 116-123</li><li>Kottelat, M. (2001). Fishes of Laos. WHT Publications, Colombo, 1-198</li><li>Pallas, P. S. (1776). Reise durch verschiedene Provinzen des Russischen Reichs. Dritter Theil. Vom Jahr 1772 und 1773. Kayserlichen Academie der Wissenschaften, St. Petersburg,, 1-454</li></ul>"
    }
